Below is a self-contained ES module function that converts a list-of-lists into a two-dimensional table using a second list as the column headers. It uses arquero to create a table object, but by default returns an array of row objects (header -> value). The function handles rows shorter than the headers by filling with a configurable value, and optionally allows extra values in rows (auto-generating headers for them) or throws on mismatch.

Code (ESM):

```javascript
import * as aq from 'arquero';

/**
 * Convert multiple lists (an array of arrays) into a 2D table using headers.
 *
 * @param {Array<Array<any>>} lists - Outer list whose elements are inner lists (rows).
 * @param {Array<string>} headers - Column headers to map values to.
 * @param {Object} [options]
 * @param {any} [options.fillMissing=null] - Value to use when a row is shorter than headers.
 * @param {boolean} [options.allowExtra=false] - If true, generate headers for extra values in a row;
 *                                               if false (default), rows longer than headers cause an error.
 * @param {boolean} [options.returnArqueroTable=false] - If true, return an arquero Table object; otherwise return array of objects.
 * @returns {Array<Object>|aq.Table} - Either an array of objects (rows) or an arquero Table (depending on option).
 *
 * Example:
 *   const lists = [[1,2], [3,4], [5]];
 *   const headers = ['a','b'];
 *   listsToTable(lists, headers) // => [{a:1,b:2},{a:3,b:4},{a:5,b:null}]
 */
export function listsToTable(lists, headers, options = {}) {
  const {
    fillMissing = null,
    allowExtra = false,
    returnArqueroTable = false,
  } = options;

  if (!Array.isArray(lists)) {
    throw new TypeError('lists must be an array of arrays');
  }
  if (!Array.isArray(headers)) {
    throw new TypeError('headers must be an array of strings');
  }

  // copy headers to avoid mutation
  const baseHeaders = headers.slice();

  // Build rows as objects mapping header -> value
  const rows = lists.map((row, rIdx) => {
    if (!Array.isArray(row)) {
      throw new TypeError(`lists[${rIdx}] must be an array`);
    }
    const obj = {};

    // If a row has more values than headers:
    if (row.length > baseHeaders.length) {
      if (!allowExtra) {
        throw new Error(
          `Row ${rIdx} has more values (${row.length}) than headers (${baseHeaders.length}). ` +
          `Set allowExtra: true to permit extra values (auto-generated headers).`
        );
      }
      // Generate additional headers for extra values (header_1, header_2,...)
      for (let i = baseHeaders.length; i < row.length; i++) {
        baseHeaders[i] = `extra_${i - (headers.length - 1)}`; // avoid clobbering earlier header indexes
      }
    }

    // Map values for the original and any generated headers
    for (let i = 0; i < Math.max(baseHeaders.length, row.length); i++) {
      const key = baseHeaders[i] ?? `col_${i}`;
      if (i < row.length) {
        obj[key] = row[i];
      } else {
        // Value missing in row; use fillMissing
        obj[key] = fillMissing;
      }
    }
    return obj;
  });

  // If caller wants an arquero table, build and return it
  if (returnArqueroTable) {
    return aq.table(rows);
  }

  // Default: return array of row objects
  return rows;
}
```

Usage examples:

- Basic:
  const lists = [[1,2], [3,4], [5]];
  const headers = ['a','b'];
  const table = listsToTable(lists, headers);
  // table -> [{a:1,b:2},{a:3,b:4},{a:5,b:null}]

- Allow extra columns:
  const lists = [[1,2,9], [3,4,8]];
  const headers = ['a','b'];
  const table = listsToTable(lists, headers, { allowExtra: true });
  // table -> [{a:1,b:2,extra_1:9}, {a:3,b:4,extra_1:8}]

- Return an arquero Table for further processing:
  const aqTable = listsToTable(lists, headers, { returnArqueroTable: true });
  // aqTable is an arquero Table; you can use aq Table methods (select, groupby, etc.)

